hello everybody and welcome back to the  second part of our live program in which  our customers and collaborators are  sharing their experience  around our own combined solution for  clinical research testing  so it is my pleasure now to introduce uh  two more speakers for this session we  will have uh  dr craig mcinton who is director at the  genomic diagnostic and bioinformatics  unit  he's also professor of the department of  pathology at the university of alabama  burning up  he will be talking about utilizing the  genexis system  to detect complex genetic variants in  human tissue spacemen  and right after this first talk we will  have another one from dr j bayani  she's principal investigator scientist  at the ontario institute for cancer  research  and she will be talking about the  evaluation of the uncle mind  comprehensive class  essay for profiling of solid tumors  so thank you both the speakers for  participating in this event  right after those presentation we will  have a q a session where where the  audience will be  in the position of asking questions to  the speakers  with no further delay now i would like  to leave the stage  to craig please go ahead with your  presentation thank you everybody for  joining  okay hi everyone my name is craig  mckinnon i'm from the  university of alabama at birmingham  here are some disclosures for today's  talk  okay so today i'm going to talk about  the genexis system  and go over the motivation that we at  uab  head for transitioning over to the  genexis and then talk about some  research cases that we ran  on the genexis when we're evaluating it  and then i'm going to finish  up with an interesting case describing a  mutation and a human lung cancer  specimen  so in a molecular oncology research lab  there's a couple of considerations you  have to take into  you have to evaluate when you're setting  up a panel one is the targets  target genes of interest for your panel  you need to  a lab needs to be able to detect a wide  range of genetic variants  this includes single nucleotide variants  insertion deletions  fusions and copy number of variations  and as we all know  some of these variants are hard to  detect in addition  a a platform that you are going to use  in your lab also should be capable of  testing  a wide range of specimens oftentimes the  specimens that are in your  biobanks or that you're working with are  very small they can be very poor quality  oftentimes coming from ffpe in which the  dna or rna is degraded the source can be  a needle biopsy a cytology specimen  cell free dna and ffvp  samples so you want to find a platform  that is really good  at handling a wide range of specimens at  some  depending on what test you're using a  lot of times your  sample isn't good enough and you might  end up rejecting  five to twenty percent of your samples  um  so given this uh it's important to have  a  um an asset that can have a lot of  specimens  so you can think about really three  different tiers of testing  uh oftentimes you might have a specimen  that's really tiny  you might only be able to do either a  single ihc test or maybe even just  evaluate one gene  such as like k ras or in ras or egfr  or if your specimen is small but  suitable for doing a panel  you might have to limit the number of  genes you can have on your panel and it  can be a combination of dna and rna  targets  allowing you to look for variants like  insertion deletions or single nucleotide  variants in the dna  or fusions with using rna or if you're  lucky enough to have a  large specimen you can do a  comprehensive panel that can include  hundreds of genes  and do the whole spectrum of mutations  including  single nucleotide variants insertion  deletions fusions and copy number  changes  at uab we had historically used a  haloplex target enrichment assay that  had a fairly high  requirement for our starting dna input  the the kit required 200 nanograms but  we had  optimized it down to 100 nanograms and  the assay did have one advantage is that  each molecule that you sequenced had a  unique molecular barcode which is  important  for being able to have high accuracy and  quality of your sequencing data  but one of the problems with this assay  was that the starting material was so  high that oftentimes we were unable to  sequence a lot of our  research samples so that was one of the  main reasons that started to  ask motivate us to look around for  another system  and we evaluated the ion torrent genexis  and it had some  features and qualities that were really  important to us one is it could work  with a wide range of tissue samples  most of our samples research samples are  ffpe  and we have a lot of blood from those  same  samples that are paired with it it works  really well with low input  dna and rna each pool that you work with  only needs 10 nanograms  it can detect depending on the on the  kit or assay you're using it can detect  a wide range of variants  including smvs indels cnvs and fusions  has very little hands-on time which is  really nice for uh for the lab  and the two features that uh really were  most uh  important to us were the fast turnaround  time of two days  and then we did some calculations and  recognized that it represented a massive  cost savings for us  um this next slide goes over our cost  savings  uh it's you see this table here and this  is an evaluation of  running uh our current panel  which is a hundred gene uh dna panel  compared to running the oncomine  comprehensive assay version three  it's a research panel that includes 163  genes  on the genexis and by doing a comparison  of the two assays we recognize that it  would be close to 300  000 a year in cost savings um  which basically would be close to paying  for the instrument within one year  in addition to the to the huge cost  savings we also got  a lot of new assays coming out of the  genexis we could do  uh rna this uh which will replace some  of our fish assays which are very  expensive to run  and less sensitive and the faster  turnaround time  of two days was very very compelling  and it also allowed us to capture a lot  of the small specimens we weren't  normally able to sequence  so during our evaluation of the ion  torrent  genexis system we looked at two of the  assays that are available the oncoming  myeloid assay  which targets 40 dna targets and 29 rna  fusion genes  and the oncoming precision assay we  found some of our old reference samples  we had previously sequenced  uh we found four specimens we wanted to  resequence  that were dna derived from blood and  three samples that were derived from  ffpe that we were going to run on the  oncoming precision assay  so for them we'll talk about the myeloid  genexis assay first  so we identified samples that had  10 mutations in 10 genes that were very  important  in different myeloid disorders and these  genes could be  categorized into different types of  pathways in the cell that are important  for cell growth  uh you're also very prevalent in  different diseases that  um affect the blood including myeloid  leukemias and myeloproliferative  diseases  and these some of these genes uh  represented variants  that were really hard to detect too  either due to the size of the variant  such as in flit 3 or calr or ones that  were just notoriously difficult to  sequence because of the  gene itself might have had a lot of gc  rich content such as with cebpa  so we thought by selecting these genes  it would really give us a  a good chance to evaluate the system and  see how well it performs  so of the four samples we had  each sample had between one  to four or five mutations in it we ran  each sample and duplicate  and so this table shows the  two of the samples the second column  shows the  mutation the genes that we were um  targeting  uh with the mutations listed and some of  the metrics from the  sequencing run are described and the  next slide  are other two s