n  we ran it in duplicate and this chart  shows that on the genexis we were able  to pick up egfr amplification  and recognize that about in each of the  samples are roughly 33  copy number amplification of the egfr  gene  now another interesting thing that came  out of this experiment is we  recognized that the same sample also had  a loss of  an allele of the cdk n2a gene  which we did not know because our assay  at uab did not target this  so in the sample there was a gain of  egfr  and loss of cdkn2a  another variant we were interested in  involves the met gene  now the diagram on the right side  shows uh in the upper part of the  diagram what happens  normally to the met gene and that's  exons 13 and 14 and 15 are normally  spliced together  and then the protein after it's  translated gets inserted to  the membrane and the levels of the met  gene are tightly controlled  because in exon 14 there's a recognition  site for the cbl  degradation system and when met levels  reach a certain level  the cbl system comes along and degrades  the met gene  product and that's how the cell  maintains  the appropriate levels of the met gene  and can control ceiling that way  now in certain tumors including lung  cancer met  exon 14 is deleted and that leads to  exon 13 being spliced adjacent to exon  15.  the consequence of this is that the cbl  recognition site is now deleted and that  leads to accumulation of met  in the membrane and increased net  signaling which  promotes cell growth so  in order to detect this event  you can take two strategies at uab we  sequence the dna  and when you do this you have to examine  both the splice acceptor and splice  donor site of the mexon met exon 14  as well as the dry sequence which is the  recognition sequence for the cbl  degradation pathway  so there's a lot of real estate that you  have to target and sometimes you miss  the mutation due to the sequencing  issues but we had a sample where we did  find the splice site  mutation and